# .easignore — explicit upload excludes for EAS Build.
#
# EAS archives the project from this file when present (falling back to
# .gitignore otherwise). Nested .gitignore files such as android/.gitignore are
# not reliably applied to the EAS archive, and a stale native build output
# leaking into the builder is not a cosmetic problem:
#
# android/build/generated/autolinking/autolinking.json caches ABSOLUTE paths
# from whichever machine last ran Gradle. A Windows-generated copy shipped to
# the Linux builder makes Gradle configure projects under paths like
#   /home/expo/workingdir/build/android/C:/Users/.../node_modules/react-native-worklets/android
# and the build dies with "Configuring project ':react-native-worklets' without
# an existing directory is not allowed."
#
# Keeping these out of the archive makes the build independent of whoever ran
# the last local Gradle command.

# --- JavaScript dependencies (installed fresh on the builder) ---
node_modules/
.expo/
.expo-shared/
dist/
web-build/
*.log

# --- Native build output: machine-specific, must never ship ---
android/build/
android/.gradle/
android/app/build/
android/app/.cxx/
android/app/src/main/java/inline/
android/app/src/main/assets/index.android.bundle
android/app/src/main/assets/*.jsbundle
android/local.properties
ios/build/
ios/Pods/
ios/.xcode.env.local

# --- Secrets and signing material ---
.env
.env.local
*.keystore
!android/app/debug.keystore
*.jks
*.p8
*.p12
*.mobileprovision
credentials.json
keystore.properties
*.keystore.properties
google-play-service-account.json
firebase-service-account.json
*firebase-adminsdk*.json
